From: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
Subject: [PATCH] serial: qcom-geni: drop a stale wakeup edge on suspend

The dedicated wake IRQ of a geni UART watches for an edge on the RX line,
and on platforms where that pin is routed through the PDC the edge latches
in hardware even while the IRQ is masked. Any traffic on the way into
suspend therefore leaves the wake IRQ pending.

dpm_suspend_noirq() arms the wake IRQs before it runs a single noirq
callback, so an edge latched earlier fires the moment the IRQ is armed:
irq_pm_check_wakeup() disables it again, marks the system as having a
wakeup pending, and freeze_secondary_cpus() bails out with "Wakeup
pending. Abort CPU freeze". Nothing clears the latch, so the next attempt
latches the same way and the machine can never suspend.

On a Fairphone 5 this is reproducible on every single attempt: the
Bluetooth controller on uart7 exchanges in-band sleep indications with the
host as the port suspends, that RX activity latches the edge, and suspend
aborts with "PM: Triggering wakeup from IRQ 154" naming the port's
99c000.serial:wakeup line. Masking the port's wakeup (or powering the
controller down) is the only way to suspend, and both cost wake-on-Bluetooth.

Clear the pending state once the port has suspended. Devices below the port
have already suspended by then -- for the case above the controller has
finished its sleep handshake -- so the line is idle and the only edge that
can be dropped is the stale one. An edge asserted after this point latches
as usual and still wakes the system, so wake-on-Bluetooth keeps working.

Signed-off-by: Marcus Ramberg <marcus@means.no>
---
diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
index a851b8f0e..7b5c6dffc 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/qcom_geni_serial.c
@@ -2105,6 +2105,22 @@ static int qcom_geni_serial_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;

+	/*
+	 * The dedicated wake IRQ watches an edge on RX and that edge latches
+	 * even while the IRQ is masked, so any traffic seen on the way down
+	 * leaves it pending. dpm_suspend_noirq() arms the wake IRQ before it
+	 * runs a single noirq callback, so an already pending edge fires at
+	 * once and aborts the suspend -- and does so on every retry, since the
+	 * next attempt latches the same way. Devices below this port have
+	 * suspended by the time we get here (a Bluetooth controller on this
+	 * UART has completed its in-band sleep handshake) and the line is
+	 * idle, so drop the stale edge. An edge asserted after this point
+	 * latches as usual and still wakes the system.
+	 */
+	if (port->wakeup_irq > 0)
+		irq_set_irqchip_state(port->wakeup_irq, IRQCHIP_STATE_PENDING,
+				      false);
+
 	/*
 	 * When no_console_suspend is set the console must remain active
 	 * across system sleep, so skip the force suspend path.
